You want to add a link to every post that will be created in the future, as well as existing posts. Since you're hosting your website using Nginx, you'll need to modify your existing configuration to achieve this.

Here are the changes you can make:

1. Create a new file `post.link` in your HTML root directory (`/usr/share/nginx/html/`):
```html
<!-- post.link -->
<a href="/posts/{{ post_id }}" class="post-link">Read more</a>
```
This file will contain a basic HTML anchor tag with a `post-link` class. The `{{ post_id }}` placeholder will be replaced with the actual post ID.

2. Modify your existing `location /` block to include the new `post.link` file:
```nginx
location / {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index index.html index.htm;
    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;

    # Add the post link to existing posts
    if (not $request_uri ~* ^/(.*)$) {
        rewrite ^(.*)$ /post.link break;
    }
}
```
This block will serve the `index.html` file as the main page and inject the `post.link` HTML snippet for each post.

3. Add a new `location /posts` block to handle post requests:
```nginx
location /posts {
    internal;
    proxy_pass http://localhost:5000/posts;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
}
```
This block will proxy requests to `http://localhost:5000/posts` (assuming your backend runs on port 5000). You can adjust the port number or URL as needed.

